\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Rollback Attacks in TEEs}

Rollback attacks and the notion of ``state continuity'' are well studied in trusted execution settings. Memoir introduced practical state continuity for protected modules, establishing the theoretical foundation for understanding rollback vulnerabilities~\cite{memoir}. ROTE provided rollback protection for trusted execution environments~\cite{rote}.

Our setting differs from TEE research: the target is an application-layer agent whose control flow and requests may be synthesized from natural language, introducing non-determinism absent in programs typically protected by TEEs.

\subsection{Agentic Systems and Exploration}

Recent systems work on agentic exploration motivates checkpoint-restore and identifies external side effects as a key unresolved challenge~\cite{agenticexploration}. This work provides the systems foundation for agent exploration but does not frame external side effects as a concrete security vulnerability with measurable invariants.

\subsection{Agent Frameworks with Checkpointing}

Agent frameworks expose checkpoint-restore and time travel as features. LangGraph provides persistent checkpoints and time-travel interfaces for agent graphs~\cite{langgraph}. These capabilities make restore and fork operations common in practice, increasing the attack surface for the vulnerabilities we describe.

\subsection{Agent Security Mechanisms}

Agent security mechanisms such as privilege-control and runtime enforcement focus on constraining tool use. Progent provides programmable privilege control for LLM agents~\cite{progent}, while AgentSpec offers customizable runtime enforcement~\cite{agentspec}. These approaches are largely orthogonal to checkpoint-restore-induced rewind and fork semantics. They constrain what actions an agent can take but do not address the state continuity problems that arise from checkpoint-restore.

\subsection{Prompt Injection and LLM Security}

OWASP documents prompt injection and insecure output handling among top risks for LLM applications~\cite{owaspllm}. While our work focuses on checkpoint-restore vulnerabilities rather than prompt injection, the two threat categories can interact: an attacker might use prompt injection to influence agent state in ways that are preserved across checkpoints.

\section{Conclusion}

Checkpoint-restore and time-travel execution are becoming standard building blocks for tool-using LLM agents, driven by operational needs such as recovery and debugging, and algorithmic needs such as exploration. Existing systems practice assumes deterministic replay or stable idempotency identities to keep external side effects safe under retry~\cite{temporal, stripeidempotency}. LLM agents weaken these assumptions: restore can rewind internal authorization and safety state while the external world does not rewind, and post-restore action synthesis can vary enough to defeat idempotency mechanisms.

This creates a measurable security problem: replay of irreversible actions, and revocation and consumption bypass. We provide a testbed design to reproduce and quantify these failures using CRIU~\cite{criu} and external tool services with append-only logs.

Our analysis reveals a tension: the features that make checkpoint-restore valuable for agent development, including state preservation and failure recovery, are precisely the features that create security vulnerabilities when agents interact with external systems that maintain their own state.

Future work should explore:
\begin{itemize}
    \item External authorization services issuing short-lived, single-use capabilities verified at tool execution time
    \item Tool-side commit logs that can detect and reject replayed requests even with varying idempotency keys
    \item Semantic-aware checkpointing that captures the logical operation identity rather than just process state
\end{itemize}


